index 0000000..492e7a2
--- /dev/null
+++ b/HEGEL_REPORT.md
@@ -0,0 +1,97 @@
+# Hegel Property-Based Testing Report — `ulid` 3.0.0
+
+## 1. Setup
+
+- Added `hegeltest` (0.28.2) as a dev-dependency (`cargo add --dev hegeltest -p ulid`;
+  the package is a workspace with a `cli` member, so `-p ulid` targets the library).
+- Tests were added to the **existing** `#[cfg(test)]` modules in `src/lib.rs`,
+  `src/generator.rs`, and `src/time.rs` — no new test files.
+- `.hegel/` (hegel's failure database) added to `.gitignore`.
+- No AI-contribution opt-out was found (no `HUMAN_VS_MACHINE.md`, no `CONTRIBUTING.md`,
+  no anti-AI clause in README/LICENSE). Nothing is being upstreamed; work is local only.
+- Baseline before changes: 30 unit tests + 24 doc tests, all green.
+
+## 2. Properties Added (13 tests, 9 distinct properties)
+
+In `src/lib.rs::tests`:
+
+| Test | Property |
+|---|---|
+| `prop_string_roundtrip` | **Crown roundtrip**: any `u128` → `to_string()` (26 chars) → `from_string`/`FromStr`/`TryFrom<&str>` recovers the same Ulid; lowercase input decodes identically (Crockford case-insensitivity); inherent `to_string` agrees with the `Display` impl. |
+| `prop_integer_conversions_roundtrip` | `u128`, big-endian `[u8; 16]`, and `(u64, u64)` conversions are bijective views of the same 128 bits, with std's `to_be_bytes`/shifts as the oracle. |
+| `prop_from_parts_roundtrip` | `from_parts(ts, rand)` keeps exactly the low 48/80 bits ("Any overflow bits ... are discarded") and `timestamp_ms()`/`random()` recover them; full-width draws boosted with in-width draws so both the masking and the exact roundtrip are exercised. |
+| `prop_from_string_rejects_wrong_length` | Any length ≠ 26 (0..=52, all-valid characters) → `Err(DecodeError::InvalidLength)`. |
+| `prop_from_string_rejects_invalid_char` | Corrupting one position of a valid encoding with a non-Crockford ASCII byte (boosting the excluded letters I/L/O/U in both cases) → `Err(DecodeError::InvalidChar)`. |
+| `prop_from_string_never_panics` | Parse robustness: arbitrary Unicode text never panics `from_string`/`parse`. |
+| `prop_valid_string_canonical_roundtrip` | Any in-range 26-char Crockford string (first char `'0'..='7'`, mixed case) decodes, and re-encoding yields its uppercase canonical form (decode is injective on in-range strings modulo case). |
+| `prop_from_string_rejects_out_of_range` | Strings above `Ulid::max()` (`7ZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZ`) — first char encoding 8..=31 — must be rejected per the ULID spec. **FAILS — real bug, see §4.** |
+| `prop_string_order_matches_value_order` | "Lexicographically sortable": `String` ordering of two encoded Ulids equals `u128` ordering. |
+| `prop_timestamp_order_implies_string_order` | A strictly larger 48-bit timestamp sorts strictly later as a string, regardless of both random parts. |
+| `prop_increment` | `increment()` bumps the random part and keeps the timestamp; at the 80-bit random maximum (boosted by construction) it returns the next-millisecond Ulid in `Err`, saturating at `Ulid::max`. |
+
+In `src/generator.rs::tests`:
+
+| Test | Property |
+|---|---|
+| `prop_generator_monotonic` | The documented guarantee "each call provides a Ulid with a larger value than the last" holds for arbitrary timestamp sequences, including repeated and *backwards* clocks; a strictly advancing clock is reflected in `timestamp_ms()`. Overflow `Err`s are committed via `commit_overflow_increment()`; saturation at `Ulid::max` is the one documented exception. |
+
+In `src/time.rs::tests`:
+
+| Test | Property |
+|---|---|
+| `prop_datetime_roundtrip` | For any representable millisecond instant (48 bits), `from_datetime_with_source(dt, ..)` reports exactly `dt` back through both `timestamp_ms()` and `datetime()`. |
+
+Generator notes: randomness for the code under test goes through the crate's own
+`StepRng`, which is a *counter*, not a seeded PRNG — the hegel-drawn `value`/`increment`
+map directly onto the random bits consumed, so counterexamples shrink meaningfully.
+The `is_crockford_byte` oracle and the `draw_ulid_string` composite are written
+independently of the crate's `LOOKUP` table.
+
+## 3. Validation (Run and Reflect)
+
+- **Liveness**: five assertions were temporarily inverted — including inside the
+  guarded `Err` arm of `prop_increment` (proving the maxed-random branch fires; it is
+  boosted by construction) and inside `prop_generator_monotonic`'s advancing-clock
+  guard — and all five tests failed with shrunk counterexamples, then were reverted.
+- **Exploratory 10x run**: all hegel tests were run once at `test_cases = 1000`
+  (default 100), verified with an atomic case counter printed from
+  `prop_string_roundtrip` ("case 1000" observed, so the raised count genuinely ran).
+  No additional failures surfaced; counts and counter reverted for the committed tests.
+- Final suite: 42 of 43 unit tests pass, 24 doc tests pass. The single failure is the
+  deliberately pinned bug below.
+
+## 4. Bugs Found
+
+**`Ulid::from_string` / `base32::decode` accepts out-of-range ULID strings and
+silently discards the two overflow bits** (`src/base32.rs`, `decode`).
+
+- Minimal counterexample (hegel-shrunk): `"80000000000000000000000000"` decodes to
+  `Ok(Ulid(0))` — i.e. it collides with `"00000000000000000000000000"`.
+- 26 Crockford base32 characters carry 130 bits; a Ulid has 128. The ULID spec
+  (which the crate's docs link as what it implements) says the largest valid ULID is
+  `7ZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZ` and "Any attempt to decode or encode a ULID larger
+  than this should be rejected by all implementations, to prevent overflow bugs".
+  `decode`'s `value = (value << 5) | val` shifts the top bits out of the `u128`
+  silently, making `from_string` non-injective (each Ulid has 4 accepted spellings
+  per case variant).
+- Verified **not** an artifact of this checkout: the crate source is byte-identical
+  to `ulid 3.0.0` from crates.io (apart from these tests), so this is genuine
+  upstream behavior violating the spec.
+- Pinned by `tests::prop_from_string_rejects_out_of_range`, left failing per
+  instructions. The failure is fully deterministic: every input the test's generator
+  draws is out of range and every one is accepted, so it fails on the first case of
+  every run (no flakiness).
+- Note: a fix would need an error variant for overflow (or reuse `InvalidChar` /
+  `InvalidLength`), since `DecodeError` currently has no fitting variant.
+
+## 5. Notes and Recommendations
+
+- The `serde`, `uuid`, `postgres`, and `rkyv` feature-gated modules were not
+  property-tested (features off by default; the serde roundtrip would be a natural
+  follow-up with the `serde` feature enabled).
+- `prop_generator_monotonic` deliberately feeds *backwards* clocks — the
+  increment-on-same-or-earlier-millisecond path (`generator.rs`) is exercised on
+  essentially every run, not just the fresh-timestamp path.
+- If the out-of-range bug is ever fixed upstream, `prop_valid_string_canonical_roundtrip`
+  already covers the accept side of the boundary (first char `'0'..='7'`), so the
+  pair of tests fences the boundary exactly at `Ulid::max()`.
